#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae460c7-0e1f-569b-bec4-226c4f1ef6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+Bt{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360639} {:answer/id #uuid "84655674-afa3-5a63-8c65-2e82b4512494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360640} {:answer/id #uuid "e39dbe18-f115-5185-bfda-4452826d62e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360641} {:answer/id #uuid "c575c40a-3b6b-590b-8f58-bd27f16719c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360642} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc8d2dc-caa5-54f8-86f7-dba73b353657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c575c40a-3b6b-590b-8f58-bd27f16719c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360642} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f33fb8-e98b-5cf5-92e4-d3998c8c60fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2fac4e5-7135-5e39-8138-1f3b4b97226e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129957" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{4t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-16y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){e}^{4t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+4u{e}^{4t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t}+16u{e}^{4t})-16u{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{4t}+8{u}^{′}{e}^{4t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}({u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}+8w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{-8t}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{e}^{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{8}{e}^{-8t}+{c}_{2}){e}^{4t}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-8$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-8t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}={e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-8\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4t}+B{e}^{-4t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "402446f4-6bf7-54bf-9b23-826e89be6924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360646} {:answer/id #uuid "b75153cf-90d4-5a3e-bded-2f36ba1f87f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b75153cf-90d4-5a3e-bded-2f36ba1f87f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360647} :answer-field/id #uuid "34067bab-9188-5585-b369-e90b291a5a61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360645} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffa027fc-da92-5e59-ba71-774056f4fb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360649} {:answer/id #uuid "206ed363-b4e7-59d5-ba5f-a5236f3f811f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa027fc-da92-5e59-ba71-774056f4fb32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360649} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea05929-b994-5fb2-b599-c15c77783264" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360648} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6830619f-acf5-5944-b32f-140244a340c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360652} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8734fb-d623-5824-a0d7-fd502aa1fdab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360653} {:answer/id #uuid "408dd2dc-8a72-5d63-ad8b-e96909057bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360654} {:answer/id #uuid "f094967c-6783-5e4f-98ab-4cb60e9a3b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f094967c-6783-5e4f-98ab-4cb60e9a3b0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360655} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d20a9f-a43f-58a5-8352-4d924179e342" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360651} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb27e9c-f6e5-5ef1-959c-817d158468b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360657} {:answer/id #uuid "4387d001-bc7d-5553-a0b3-d3ce8346c924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Cx{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360658} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1ef7db-63dd-5c60-900e-b6cedecc1059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360659} {:answer/id #uuid "42d1c719-f552-5d23-9165-8017325be45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d1c719-f552-5d23-9165-8017325be45d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360660} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea77bb7-3c11-52c5-96cc-93ae987cb1aa" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f003350-cf55-5f01-8845-98a1c5fc423f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360662} {:answer/id #uuid "b521df7b-d913-5aec-8a35-fceae06c08d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f003350-cf55-5f01-8845-98a1c5fc423f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360662} :answer-field/id #uuid "52d2d7b7-680e-5cb0-9558-6f38383fab21" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "703a7a55-d89b-5466-a847-c7db23ef2a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-339879" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y=-12{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{-2x})^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(-2{e}^{-2x})^{′}=4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(4{e}^{-2x})+5(-2{e}^{-2x})-6({e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{-2x})^{′}={e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})^{′}=-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(-4{e}^{-2x}+4x{e}^{-2x})+5({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x})-6(x{e}^{-2x})=-12{e}^{-2x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-6x}+{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8d93e63-ee9f-5c7d-bd77-ae65ac577d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360666} {:answer/id #uuid "ad907199-f13a-5f57-b5e6-e24f4579d028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+B{e}^{-x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360667} {:answer/id #uuid "4c72ef9b-4587-5060-bed7-8b3b781c9a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360668} {:answer/id #uuid "f33e58a8-d0f4-54fa-9af7-2068f0cbcbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+B{e}^{x/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360669} {:answer/id #uuid "c34b8e6f-3b2d-5272-a328-1794ea50aaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{-x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c72ef9b-4587-5060-bed7-8b3b781c9a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360668} :answer-field/id #uuid "89eec56d-e3c9-5f1f-9751-f0df759ff987" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25f76d61-f5bd-5300-a8bb-fba17cb3162f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79932" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/2}$ is a solution to the equation $4{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{4}{e}^{x/2})-4({u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{u}{2}{e}^{x/2})+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2}-4{u}^{′}{e}^{x/2}-2u{e}^{x/2}+u{e}^{x/2} & =0 \\\\ 4{u}^{″}{e}^{x/2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{e}^{x/2}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x/2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}({c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x/2}\\cdot x=x{e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={e}^{x}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "579e8f34-c144-5f56-99b3-f9d9d7e2a4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360673} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1f53c9-5627-58c5-b1cd-8d0e7465676e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{-3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360674} {:answer/id #uuid "666defca-4628-53b7-ab19-517f60e736f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360675} {:answer/id #uuid "a048e4a0-9260-5af6-8718-630f35b6372a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360676} {:answer/id #uuid "e24316b7-d6b2-5c8c-9234-8f29428f44ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a048e4a0-9260-5af6-8718-630f35b6372a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360676} :answer-field/id #uuid "268af40b-d4fd-56b6-b810-7de22bde78dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3624b381-ff5b-5c61-a5d8-e12407af8abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-79935" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation $2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+2y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-3x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+2(u{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+8{u}^{′}{x}^{3}+4u{x}^{2}-3{u}^{′}{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{2}+2u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{u}^{″}{x}^{4}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n2x{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n2x{w}^{′}+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5/2}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(-\\frac{2{c}_{1}}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+{c}_{2}){x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=({x}^{-3/2}){x}^{2}={x}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{2}+B{x}^{1/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "028a880e-33f9-5152-9520-1f3ddd7e6c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360680} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec54203-c9ef-58d3-9bfe-87f2f076f65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+B{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360681} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2da10b-0985-558d-826d-eac0a9c976c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360682} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5e5b0e-8ff9-57fd-9029-4ca94e849004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360683} {:answer/id #uuid "a37c98b0-7487-5ee9-92a5-4144dd85344c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e5e5b0e-8ff9-57fd-9029-4ca94e849004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360683} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b787034-cdbd-54cb-884d-5854355abc58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c467ed53-f0ee-5801-b4aa-ed3fa391372c" :question/math-academy-id "q-90763" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx{w}^{′}+6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-5\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}]}
